blocks.body:
Let $f : X \to X$ be a continuous map of a metric space. Then $f$ is chaotic on $X$ if

1. periodic points of $f$ are dense in $X$;
2. $f$ is topologically transitive: for any nonempty open sets $U, V$ there is $n \ge 0$ with $f^n(U) \cap V \ne \varnothing$;
3. $f$ has sensitive dependence on initial conditions: there is $\delta > 0$ such that for every $x$ and every neighbourhood $U$ of $x$ there are $y \in U$ and $n \ge 0$ with $d(f^n x, f^n y) \ge \delta$.

In words: the motion is rich in regular-looking cycles, mixes every region with every other, and amplifies arbitrarily small errors to a fixed size. (For flows, a common working definition is: bounded, aperiodic long-term behaviour of a deterministic system with a positive Lyapunov exponent.)

